<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acarnania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acarnania is a region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a>, the Achelous river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>. In antiquity its people, the Acarnanians, lived in small towns joined in a federal league centred on Stratos, and they were long allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> against Corinth's colonies and later with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>. It is now part of the regional unit of Aetolia-Acarnania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acarnania.jpg" alt="Acarnania" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:el:Χρήστης:Lord Makro · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stratos,_Etolia_Acarnania,_Greece_-_Ancient_theater.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Acarnania lay in the westernmost part of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> and north of the gulf of Calydon, the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. It was reckoned the most westerly province of Greece and covered about 4,070 square kilometres, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> and Amphilochia to the north and the Achelous River as its boundary with Aetolia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. It had a rocky coastline, a rugged strip of mountains along the coast, and plains between the mountains and the Achelous. The soil was fertile but little cultivated, and the chief wealth of the inhabitants lay in herds and flocks pastured in the meadows of the lower Achelous. Pliny mentions iron mines and a pearl-fishery off Actium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Greek mythology the land was founded by Acarnan, son of Alcmaeon. Its early inhabitants were said to be the Taphii, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leleges">Leleges</a> and the Curetes. In the 7th century BCE Corinth settled Anactorium, Sollium and Leucas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Kefalonia</a> settled Astacus, driving the earlier inhabitants into the interior. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> the Acarnanians, who lived mostly in dispersed villages and made good light-armed troops and slingers, were bound in the Acarnanian League, whose meetings were usually held at Stratus. It had a council, a general assembly and a general at its head.</p><p>Hatred of the Corinthian settlers led them to side with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and about 432 BCE they joined it in restoring the Amphilochians to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. In 426 BCE they won a victory under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> over the Peloponnesians and Ambraciots at the Battle of Olpae. They later joined the Second Athenian League in 375 BCE and fought with Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> at Chaeronea. In 314 BCE, at the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, settlements near the Aetolian border were merged into fewer, larger ones, and about 250 BCE the territory was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>.</p><p>Acarnania then drew close to the Antigonid kings and stayed loyal to Philip V in his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, submitting only after the capture of Leucas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a>. In 191 BCE they were persuaded by Mnasilochus to side with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, and after 168 BCE Thyrreion replaced Leucas as capital. In the 1st century BCE the country suffered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and Roman civil wars, and Augustus moved the inhabitants of several towns to his new city of Nicopolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 395 Acarnania became part of the Eastern Roman Empire. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204 it passed to the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, in 1348 it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and in 1480 it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> since 1832 and forms the western part of the regional unit of Aetolia-Acarnania.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acarnania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Acarnania</b>, between the Ambracian Gulf and the Achelous river, was a land of small towns and villages.</blockquote><a href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Peloponnesian War it fought the <b>Acarnanians</b> and Amphilochian Argos, and in 426 BCE the Athenian general Demosthenes destroyed its army at Idomene.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a></li><li><blockquote>Corinthians led by a son of the tyrant Cypselus founded Leucas c. 640 BCE on what was then a peninsula of <b>Acarnania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/leucas">Leucas</a></li></ul></section>
